# Line endings are fixed here so that a build is reproducible.
#
# Without this the checkout decides: git's core.autocrlf, which defaults to
# true on Windows, converts text files on the way out, and the build tool
# preserves whatever it reads. The same commit then produced a different
# bundle on each platform, measured at 2,451,396 bytes on Linux against
# 2,533,278 on Windows for script/dragonfly.js, the difference being the
# carriage returns. TFlavius/opera#1160 has to produce that bundle
# reproducibly and TFlavius/opera#1162 verifies a published hash against it,
# so neither can work while the bytes depend on where the checkout happened.
#
# LF rather than CRLF because that is what the overwhelming majority of the
# tree already is, and because the client is served to a browser rather than
# opened in a Windows editor.
#
# The source files carry a UTF-8 BOM by design; the build verifies it with
# verify_bom. Normalising line endings does not touch a BOM.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Images are declared rather than left to the heuristic, so that a file whose
# bytes happen to look like text is never rewritten.
*.png binary
*.gif binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.woff binary
*.ttf binary
